# MCSP Determination Boundary 0.15

Status: Constructive kernel experiment
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Parent: Anonymous Condition Kernel 0.14

## Problem

0.14 asks:

What makes a CONDITION available without already interpreting it?

MCSP SHALL NOT answer this by assigning an object, name, value, state, type, position, order, taxonomy, or host encoding.

MCSP also SHALL NOT make an unrestricted metaphysical existence claim.

## Determination hypothesis

Introduce a provisional distinction:

CONDITION
    pre-semantic candidate

DETERMINATION
    limitation on admissible interpretation

Determination does not state what the condition means.

It only requires that not every interpretation is equally admissible.

This supplies non-vacuity without supplying semantic product.

## Kernel boundary

Provisional kernel relation:

    CONDITION | DETERMINATION

The vertical bar is documentary notation only. It does not yet assert direction, order, identity, causation, or binary opposition.

The intended requirement is:

A condition becomes usable by MCSP only insofar as interpretation is constrained.

No constraint means no qualified semantic claim.

## Negative definition

DETERMINATION is not automatically:
- truth;
- value;
- identity;
- state;
- measurement;
- observation;
- physical cause;
- instruction;
- encoding;
- name;
- taxonomy;
- temporal event.

Any of these may later realize or interpret determination.

## Interpretation

Interpretation may introduce a semantic claim only when its asserted distinctions are supported by determination.

Therefore:

    arbitrary assertion
        is not qualified interpretation

and:

    pre-written semantic rule
        is not sufficient by itself

A canonical semantic rule still requires an applicable determination when projected onto a realization.

## Semantic emergence

Provisional dependency:

    CONDITION
      + DETERMINATION
        -> admissible distinction

    admissible distinctions
        -> relations

    qualified relations
        -> identity / position / order / scope

    interpreted configuration
        -> STATE

    ordered interpreted conditions
        -> TRANSITION

    qualified relation graph
        -> SEMANTICS

This is a hypothesis for reduction, not a mandatory construction sequence.

## Why CONDITION is not STATE

STATE asserts an interpreted configuration.

CONDITION at the kernel boundary does not.

A state can therefore be projected from conditions, but the kernel condition is not forced to inherit the state model.

## Why DETERMINATION is not CONSTRAINT

CONSTRAINT is already an interpreted semantic role limiting admissible states or transitions.

DETERMINATION is proposed earlier than those concepts.

If determination ultimately requires the full semantics of constraint, this hypothesis fails and must be rolled back.

## Realization witnesses

A realization may supply evidence of determination through physical, logical, protocol, or computational behavior.

The witness does not define the kernel semantics.

Examples are deliberately non-authoritative: voltage discrimination, stored charge, register behavior, protocol field discrimination, mechanical position, or an emulator's modeled condition may each witness determination under a realization.

MCSP semantics remain independent of which witness is used.

## Driver consequence

A driver author need not describe the complete ontology of a device.

The driver needs enough realization evidence to determine the distinctions on which its invoked semantics depend.

Thus:

    device evidence
      -> determination
      -> qualified distinction
      -> selected semantics
      -> OS projection

Unknown device conditions may remain uninterpreted.

MCSP SHALL permit that incompleteness.

## Canonical consequence

A canonical is not made true by its definition alone.

A canonical supplies a stable semantic relation graph.

Its application to a realization requires determination sufficient to support the graph's required distinctions.

This separates:

    semantic definition
from
    semantic applicability

## Tests

D1 Product-free:
Determination must not prescribe the resulting meaning.

D2 Non-vacuous:
At least one interpretation must be rejectable when unsupported.

D3 Name-free:
Determination must not require an intrinsic label.

D4 Value-free:
Determination must not require a predefined value domain.

D5 State-free:
Determination must precede interpreted STATE.

D6 Order-free:
Determination must not require temporal precedence.

D7 Position-free:
Determination must not require a coordinate space.

D8 Host-free:
No realization witness may become the universal definition.

D9 Extensible:
Previously unknown conditions may acquire later interpretations without rewriting the kernel.

D10 Canonical-safe:
New extension semantics may not mutate an existing invoked canonical.

D11 Driver-local:
A realization may qualify only the semantic subset it needs.

D12 Self-description:
MCSP must eventually represent determination and these qualification rules using MCSP itself.

## Residue

The following remain unresolved:

    limitation
    admissible
    support
    applicability
    interpretation

These terms are not silently accepted as final primitives.

## Current hypothesis

The kernel may require neither a named object nor a predefined state.

It may require only:

    an anonymous CONDITION
    with sufficient DETERMINATION
    for a claimed interpretation to be non-arbitrary.

Whether CONDITION and DETERMINATION can be reduced to one self-consistent kernel form remains open.
